> h(x: u8 in 0..3) = match x { 0 => [1,2][0], 1 => [1,2][1], _ => [1,2][2] }
> show h(0)
_() = 1
  -> int
> show h(1)
_() = 2
  -> int
> show h(2)
_() = undefined
  -> int
> show h(3)
_() = undefined
  -> int
> f(x: u8 in 0..5) = [10,20,30][x mod 3]
> range f
range of f -> int:
  10..30
  fits in u5
  over all 6 points of the domain
> domain f
domain of f:
  x: u8, 0..5
  requires 0 <= x % 3 < 3  (table index)
  defined at 6 of 6 points
  over all 6 points of the domain
> g(x: u8) = [10,20,30][x] where x < 2
> domain g
domain of g:
  x: u8, 0..255
  where x < 2
  requires 0 <= x < 3  (table index)
  defined at 2 of 2 points
  over all 2 points of the domain
> range g
range of g -> int:
  10..20
  fits in u5
  over all 2 points of the domain
> w(x: u8 in 0..3) = [10,20,30][x] where [1,0,1][x] == 1
> domain w
domain of w:
  x: u8, 0..3
  where [1, 0, 1][x] == 1
  requires 0 <= x < 3  (table index)
  defined at 2 of 2 points
  over all 2 points of the domain
> range w
range of w -> int:
  10..30
  fits in u5
  over all 2 points of the domain
> pm(x: u4 in 0..2) = match x { 0 => 5, 1 => 6 }
  note: match has no default arm: other values are outside the domain
> domain pm
domain of pm:
  x: u4, 0..2
  requires ((!(x == 0) && !(x == 1))) => an arm of match/piecewise covers the input
  defined at 2 of 3 points
  defined for: 0..1
  undefined e.g. at x=2
  over all 3 points of the domain
